L = lambda t, est, html: {"title": t, "est": est, "html": html}
Q = lambda q, opts, ans, why, src: {"q": q, "opts": opts, "ans": ans, "why": why, "src": src}

LESSONS = [
L("Chapter 1 — Selling Settings I: identity & defaults", "14", "<p>Selling Settings is where selling policy becomes system behaviour, and this module opens with its full treatment because everything advanced stands on it. Two chapters: identity and defaults here; the discipline switches next. A consultant should be able to defend every switch on this screen to a client's managing director — this pair of chapters is that defence, rehearsed.</p><p><b>Customer Naming By — the day-one identity decision.</b> <i>Customer Name</i> makes the typed name the record's permanent ID (Alpha Pharmacy Ltd is both label and key); <i>Naming Series</i> issues CUST-00042-style IDs with the name as display. The decision rule, in full: <b>portfolios with duplicate or near-duplicate names need the series.</b> Nigerian trade is dense with them — an Adepoju Stores in three cities, a De Divine Pharmacy twice in one LGA, chains whose branches trade under near-identical registrations. Name-keyed records collide, and the workarounds (Alpha Pharmacy 2; Alpha Pharmacy - Ikeja one) are corruption of the identity layer itself. Series-keyed records are unambiguous forever while the display name stays human.</p><p><b>Why it is a DAY-ONE decision.</b> The record's ID threads through every document, link and report that ever references it. Switching schemes after transactions exist is not a settings change — it is a data-migration project (re-keying, re-linking, testing), the kind of surgery your team performs for clients who decided casually and lived with collisions for two years first. The consultant's job at implementation is to ask the portfolio question — how many customers, how similar their names, how fast the growth — and decide ONCE, in writing, in the configuration workbook.</p><p><b>The defaults trio.</b> Default <b>Customer Group</b>, default <b>Territory</b>, default <b>Selling Price List</b>: what new records and documents pre-fill with when nobody chooses. The doctrine from Selling 1-2, restated as configuration: point each at the genuinely commonest LEAF — never the tree root, never a Misc landfill — so hurried entry lands correctly; then pair the safety net with the weekly new-record review that catches what fell into it. Defaults are the difference between lazy data entry producing usable records and producing archaeology.</p><p><b>The reflex this pair of chapters builds.</b> When a client asks <i>can the system make our people do X</i> or <i>stop them doing Y</i>, open this screen before answering anything. Most selling-policy questions are already switches; the expensive consultant is the one who scopes a customisation for a checkbox — and the credible one is the one who can also say which questions this screen does NOT answer, which is what the rest of this module maps.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — Selling Settings II: the discipline switches", "15", "<p>Four switches carry most of the policy weight, and each maps to a question clients actually ask. Learn them as question-and-switch pairs.</p><p><b>Can we stop staff typing special prices? → untick Allow User to Edit Price List Rate.</b> With it off, rates come ONLY from Item Price records — the rate field is read-only on documents for restricted roles. This is the strongest anti-discount control in existence: stronger than memos (forgotten), smaller price lists (worked around), or threats (negotiated). Pair it with the Selling 2 consequence: prices then move through exactly one door — the owned import workflow — and the firm can answer who changed what, when, from what to what, in one minute. The nuance a consultant adds: negotiation does not die; it moves to where Selling 6's governance ladder wants it — pricing rules for policy, escalation for exceptions — and the switch is often applied per role, leaving leads a band while the floor holds firm.</p><p><b>Can we prevent selling below cost? → Validate Selling Price Against Purchase Rate.</b> The below-cost block at the keyboard: a rate under the item's purchase cost refuses at entry. Margin protection where it is cheapest — before the document exists — rather than where it is customary — in a month-end report after the goods have shipped. Its honest limitation, stated to clients: it guards against the accidental and the casual, not the determined (who escalates properly through the ladder), and cost accuracy upstream is its fuel — garbage purchase rates make it a random number generator, which is a data-hygiene conversation, not a reason to leave the floor open.</p><p><b>Can the agreed rate change between order and invoice? → Maintain Same Rate Throughout Sales Cycle, plus its ACTION.</b> The switch enforces that the Sales Order's rate cannot silently differ on the Delivery Note or Sales Invoice made from it — the drift the customer experiences as betrayal (₦850 signed, ₦880 billed). Its companion <b>action setting</b> chooses the response to a detected drift: <b>Stop</b> (hard block — strict shops, fixed-price trade) or <b>Warning</b> (allow but flag — shops with legitimate mid-cycle repricing, who then own their flags). Neither silence nor auto-correction is on the menu; the consultant's question to the client is simply which failure they prefer to be expensive: a blocked document at a busy moment, or a flagged drift someone must review.</p><p><b>Same item twice on one order? → Allow Item to be Added Multiple Times.</b> The free-goods enabler (Selling 3's two-row treatment) and the two-rates-one-document case. It lives HERE — the address is itself an exam-grade fact.</p><p><b>And the global over-delivery allowance</b> — the fallback under Selling 4's per-item tolerance: the house default for item classes that never got a specific number. Set it low; let bulk classes override upward deliberately.</p><p><b>The change-control coda.</b> These switches move BEHAVIOUR, so they change like policy, not like preferences: decided with the client's management, dated, announced to the floor, never toggled casually mid-quarter to ease one loud complaint — Chapter 9 meets the shop where the edit-rate hole was quietly reopened for a friend, and what it cost.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — Product Bundles I: construction & mechanics", "14", "<p>You price as ONE thing but pick as MANY things: the Pharmacy Starter Pack at ₦450,000 that is really shelving, a scanner, and a printer. The <b>Product Bundle</b> is the correct model, and this chapter builds one and traces it through every document.</p><p><b>The construction, in order:</b></p><ul><li><b>The parent item.</b> A normal item record BUT <b>non-stock: Maintain Stock UNTICKED</b>. The parent never has warehouse quantity — it is a commercial identity, not a physical one. Is Sales Item ticked (it must appear on selling documents); item group per the tree (a Bundles or Kits leaf keeps mix reporting honest); description written for the buyer, since it IS the one line they will read.</li><li><b>The bundle definition.</b> The Product Bundle record: the parent on top, the <b>child items with quantities</b> beneath — 4 × shelf unit, 1 × scanner, 1 × printer. The children are ordinary stock items with ordinary bins.</li><li><b>The price.</b> An Item Price for the PARENT on the selling list: ₦450,000 (Selling 2's machinery, one atom). Children need no selling price for the bundle to function — their prices, if any, serve their standalone sales.</li></ul><p><b>The mechanics at each document — the part to recite:</b> on the quotation, the order, and the <b>invoice, the customer sees ONE line at the parent's price</b> — clean commercial presentation, no per-child price exposure, no arithmetic for procurement to pick at. On the <b>Delivery Note, the bundle EXPLODES</b>: the packed-items section lists the children with their quantities, and <b>it is the CHILDREN that move stock</b> — 4 shelves, 1 scanner, 1 printer deducted from their bins; the parent deducts nothing, having nothing. Price lives on the parent; picking lives on the children. Serial-tracked children (the scanner) demand their serials on the packed rows exactly as Selling 5 taught — bundling changes presentation, never identity discipline.</p><p><b>Availability.</b> A bundle is sellable as far as its SCARCEST child allows: 40 shelves, 3 scanners, 20 printers in stock means 3 packs, not 10 — the scanner is the binding constraint. Reading bundle availability is reading child bins with the definition in hand, and stocking for a bundle promotion means stocking its constraint (Chapter 4 operationalises this).</p><p><b>The rejected workaround, convicted once more.</b> A stock-tracked starter-pack item with quantity of its own is a shelf lie: nobody counts packs, the children's bins and the pack's bin double-represent the same physical goods, and every stocktake becomes a reconciliation between fictions. The whole point of the bundle object is one physical truth (children in bins) wearing one commercial face (the parent's line and price).</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — Product Bundles II: design & operations", "14", "<p>Chapter 3 built the object; this chapter is the judgment around it — when a bundle is the right model, what its margin really is, and how it behaves under operational stress.</p><p><b>Bundle vs variant vs separate lines — the placement test.</b> Three lookalikes, three different objects. A <b>variant</b> (Selling 2, Chapter 3) is ONE product in versions — the polo in five sizes: one template, size children, each a stock item. A <b>bundle</b> is SEVERAL products sold as one line — priced together, picked separately. And sometimes the honest answer is <b>separate lines on the quotation</b> — when the customer negotiates the components individually, itemised billing serves the relationship better than a sealed price. The test in one sentence: variants VARY one thing; bundles COMBINE many things; and when the combination is negotiable per component, do not seal it.</p><p><b>Bundle margin — the arithmetic nobody does until it hurts.</b> The parent has a price; the COST is the sum of the children's costs. Margin on the ₦450,000 pack = 450,000 minus (4 × shelf cost + scanner cost + printer cost) — and it MOVES whenever any child's cost moves, silently, because no single item record shows it. The discipline: bundles get a margin review on the same cadence as price lists (Selling 2's repricing rhythm), and Validate-Against-Purchase-Rate (Chapter 2) does not fully guard them — the parent, being non-stock, has no purchase rate of its own — so the bundle margin file is a deliberate, owned artefact. Bundles are where firms most often discover they have been selling below cost for a quarter, precisely because the loss hides in the sum.</p><p><b>Stock planning for the constraint.</b> Availability equals the scarcest child (Chapter 3), so a bundle promotion is a procurement event for its binding constraint: launching 50 packs means the scanner order lands FIRST, and the reorder levels on children carry the bundle's demand, not just their standalone runs. The operational tell of unplanned bundles: shelves of three children and a stockout on the fourth, with sales blocked by the cheapest component.</p><p><b>Returns of bundles.</b> The customer returns a pack — or one child of it. The stock half follows Selling 5 exactly: the return DN reverses the CHILDREN (all of them for a full pack; the specific child for a partial — the scanner back to quarantine by serial), because children are what moved. The money half follows Selling 7: the credit note against the invoice's parent line — full for a full return; for a partial, the commercial decision (proportional credit, or replacement of the child) is made explicitly and papered, because the parent's sealed price has no official per-child split. Mature firms keep an internal component-value split for the parent precisely to make partial-return credits non-arbitrary — another owned artefact of running bundles seriously.</p><p><b>Where bundles shine.</b> Hampers and seasonal packs, starter and installation kits, promo combinations — anywhere the offer is genuinely one decision for the buyer. Model it once, correctly, and the bundle is a pricing weapon; model it as a fake stock item and it is a stocktake dispute with a marketing name.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — Blanket Orders: the consumed agreement", "14", "<p>A customer commits to 10,000 cartons over six months at ₦820. More than an enquiry, less than a truck-now order: it is an AGREEMENT TO BE CONSUMED, and the <b>Blanket Order</b> is its instrument. This chapter is its complete lifecycle.</p><p><b>Creation.</b> The blanket order carries the customer, the items with <b>agreed quantities and agreed rates</b>, and the <b>from/to dates</b> of the agreement. Submitting it creates NO reservation and moves NO stock — deliberately: the commitment is commercial, not logistical, and the warehouse plans from the drawn orders, not the frame. (Contrast Selling 4: a Sales Order reserves because it is a delivery promise; the blanket promises a RELATIONSHIP of deliveries.)</p><p><b>Drawing against it.</b> Each real purchase is an ordinary <b>Sales Order referencing the blanket</b>, and two things happen by construction: the row <b>inherits the agreed ₦820</b> — nobody renegotiates, nobody forgets the deal rate, no override fingerprints needed because the rate is the agreement's — and the blanket records the <b>drawdown</b>: ordered-so-far against committed. From the draw onward, everything is Selling 4-5-7 as normal: the order reserves, its DNs deduct, its invoices bill at the inherited rate.</p><p><b>Monitoring the consumption.</b> The remaining commitment is read <b>from the blanket order itself</b> — ordered-vs-committed — and reviewing it monthly is account management with teeth: a customer at 20% drawdown four months into six is not going to make 10,000, and that conversation (extend? revise? let it lapse?) happens NOW, commercially, rather than as a surprise at expiry. Conversely a customer at 90% in month three earns the next-tier conversation early. The blanket turns volume promises from toasts into numbers.</p><p><b>Expiry and renewal.</b> The to-date ends the agreement: draws after it do not inherit, and the lapsed blanket stands in history as the record of what was promised and what was actually taken — the single most honest input to the NEXT negotiation (last agreement: committed 10,000, drew 7,400). Renewal is a NEW blanket at newly agreed numbers; extending old paper to house a new deal muddies both, the same law as reopened orders (Selling 4) and resurrected quotations (Selling 3).</p><p><b>The instrument comparison, final form.</b> One giant Sales Order for 10,000: reserves half a warehouse for months and lies about delivery intent. A six-month-validity quotation: an offer awaiting an answer impersonating an agreement being consumed — wrong semantics, no drawdown, no rate enforcement on draws. A pricing rule at ₦820: gives the rate but tracks no commitment and expires without a record of performance. The blanket is the only instrument that is simultaneously a rate lock, a volume ledger, and a renewal brief — which is why your Montaigne-class volume relationships belong on it.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — Sales Person: the internal team", "14", "<p><b>Sales Person</b> models your own force — and models it as a <b>tree</b>, because organisations report upward: Sales Director → Regional Managers → Field Reps. Results roll up the hierarchy automatically; the Lagos number is its reps' sum, the company number is the regions' — no spreadsheet ever built, no argument about whose figure is whose, because the figure is computed from the same documents that raised the invoices.</p><p><b>Designing the tree.</b> The same three rules as every tree in this manual (Selling 1-2): one question (WHO on our side owns this revenue), mirror how management actually reviews (if the MD reviews by region, regions are the branches), and leaves assignable in two seconds (every rep is exactly one node). And the law Selling 1 Chapter 3 established from the other side: people live in THIS tree so that geography can live in Territory — when Tunde resigns, his node is deactivated and his accounts reassigned, while Ikeja remains exactly where it was. Trees that outlive their staff are the point.</p><p><b>Assignment and contribution.</b> Selling documents carry a <b>Sales Team</b> section: one or MORE sales persons, each with a <b>contribution %</b>, <b>summing to 100</b>. The solo case is one row at 100. The worked deal splits honestly — 60/40 for the rep who ran it and the colleague who closed the technical objections — and reports credit each with their contributed share of the revenue, <b>never double-counting</b> the deal. The alternatives are all worse: two invoices for one sale (fragments the customer's paper), remarks (no report reads them), or the quiet convention that whoever types the order gets the credit (which is how CRM data becomes politics).</p><p><b>Targets.</b> Each sales person carries targets by <b>item group and period</b> — ₦30m of Pharma for Q4 — and actuals accrue from their assigned transactions. Targets aimed through the item-group tree make product-mix strategy personal: the rep whose target is weighted toward Services sells Services. The governance fact that makes targets real rather than theatre: <b>assignment precedes measurement</b> — a target against transactions nobody assigned is a number floating over a void, and Chapter 9 meets target theatre in the wild.</p><p><b>The report.</b> The <b>Sales Person-wise Transaction Summary</b>: each person's transactions, contributed amounts, performance against target — the league table, the commission basis, and the substance of the performance conversation. The honest consultant's sentence, verbatim, for every client who asks for sales-by-rep: <i>only if sales persons are assigned on the transactions — the report is arithmetic over assignments; the discipline precedes the dashboard.</i> Make the Sales Team section expected in the client's process (mandatory where their policy warrants), and the question answers itself weekly forever.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — Sales Partner: the external channel", "13", "<p>A <b>Sales Partner</b> is a business OUTSIDE your company that brings you deals — a reseller, a referral agent, a channel distributor, an implementation firm that sources clients. The record carries the partner's identity, type, and the operative field: a <b>commission rate</b>. This chapter is the channel run properly.</p><p><b>On transactions.</b> Select the partner on a Sales Order or Sales Invoice and two things happen: the deal is <b>stamped as that channel's business</b> — channel revenue becomes a reportable dimension like any other — and the document computes the <b>total commission</b> at the partner's rate: the liability you owe for the introduction, calculated per deal, automatically, at the rate the record holds rather than the rate anyone remembers.</p><p><b>The distinction to keep razor-sharp: Person vs Partner.</b> <b>Sales Person is internal</b> — your staff, a tree, contribution percentages summing to 100, targets, performance management. <b>Sales Partner is external</b> — another business, a flat entity, a commission percentage, a payable relationship. They are not two names for one idea; commission is not the divider (both structures can drive earnings); and a partner is not a customer (they bring buyers; they are not the buyer). One transaction can lawfully carry BOTH: your rep (Sales Person, 100%) worked the deal a referral firm (Sales Partner, 5%) introduced — the rep is measured, the partner is owed, and neither fact crowds the other.</p><p><b>Paying the channel.</b> Commissions settle from the <b>Sales Partner-wise Transaction Summary</b> — the system's own totals of each partner's stamped business and accrued commission. The rule with money attached: <b>pay partners from the report, never from their claims.</b> When the partner's invoice says ₦2.4m and your summary says ₦2.1m, the summary — built from the actual stamped transactions — is the negotiating position, and the gap is almost always deals the partner counts that were never stamped (a stamping-discipline conversation) or rates remembered differently (the record settles it). Channel programs die two deaths — over-payment and under-tracking — and the report prevents both.</p><p><b>Channel governance.</b> Three habits make a partner program an asset: <b>stamp at entry</b> (the partner goes on the order when the deal arrives, not reconstructed at commission time); <b>review quarterly</b> (each partner's volume, commission, and — using Selling 3's lost-reason lens — the quality of what they bring); and <b>paper the terms</b> (rate, scope, exclusivities in the partner agreement, with the record's rate matching the paper). A channel run on these three is scalable revenue; run on WhatsApp and goodwill, it is a dispute subscription.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Case study: the advanced layer, end to end", "16", "<p>One firm — recognisably shaped like a Nigerian retail-systems vendor — hardens its advanced layer in a single quarter. Every chapter clocks in; this is the module executed as a project.</p><p><b>The starting state.</b> Growing customer file with name collisions beginning; rates editable by everyone; a starter pack modelled as a fake stock item nobody counts; a big distributor buying on remembered handshake rates; sales-by-rep answered by anecdote; one referral firm paid whatever it invoices.</p><p><b>Step 1 — identity & switches (Ch. 1-2).</b> Customer Naming By moves to <b>series</b> — decided in writing before the file doubles again; defaults pointed at real leaves. The discipline set: edit-rate OFF for the floor (leads keep a band), validate-against-purchase ON, same-rate action set to <b>Stop</b> (fixed-price trade), the global over-delivery fallback at 2% with serialized classes at zero. Announced, dated, owned — and the first week's blocked below-cost quote is celebrated in standup, not resented: the floor held.</p><p><b>Step 2 — the pack done right (Ch. 3-4).</b> The fake stock item is retired; a proper bundle replaces it: non-stock parent Retail Starter Pack at ₦450,000, definition of 4 shelves + scanner + printer, parent priced on the standard list. The margin file is built (current children's costs sum to ₦361,000 — 19.8% margin, reviewed monthly); the scanner is identified as the availability constraint and its reorder level raised before the promo. First sale: one clean line on the invoice, three children exploding on the DN, the scanner's serial captured. A month later a damaged printer comes back: child returned by the Selling 5 mechanics, proportional credit issued from the internal component split — non-arbitrary because the split existed.</p><p><b>Step 3 — the handshake becomes a blanket (Ch. 5).</b> The distributor's ₦820-whenever-we-order is papered as a blanket order: 10,000 cartons, six months, the rate on the agreement. Draws inherit ₦820 untouched; month-two review shows 31% drawdown — on pace, noted. The account conversation now has a number instead of a vibe, and next renewal will negotiate from recorded performance.</p><p><b>Step 4 — the team tree (Ch. 6).</b> Sales Person tree: Director → Lagos / South-West → five reps. Sales Team made expected on orders; the first split deal books 60/40 and both reps see their contributed revenue in the summary. Q4 targets set by item group — the Services-weighted target for the rep carrying the support-plan push. The dashboard the MD wanted for a year exists in week one, because assignment finally does.</p><p><b>Step 5 — the channel papered (Ch. 7).</b> The referral firm gets a Sales Partner record at the agreed 5%, terms papered, stamping-at-entry instituted. Quarter-end: the partner invoices ₦2.4m; the summary says ₦2.1m; the gap is two deals never stamped (fixed forward) — paid ₦2.1m plus the corrected stamps, relationship intact BECAUSE the number had a source.</p><p><b>Quarter-end reads.</b> Settings holding (zero below-cost incidents, three Stop events all legitimate); bundle margin drifted to 18.1% on a shelf-cost rise — repriced; blanket at 64% with six weeks left — extension conversation opened early; team summary driving the first data-based commission run; channel report reconciled in one meeting. The advanced layer, installed once, now runs itself — which is the entire point of Chapter 9's law.</p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Support patterns & the seventh law", "14", "<p>The advanced layer's scar tissue — and the close of the manual. Symptom → disease → fix, then the law that seals the track.</p><p><b>Pattern 1 — special prices are back.</b> The edit-rate hole quietly reopened (Ch. 2) — usually for one loud friend-of-the-house, then for everyone. Fix: close it, route the friend through the ladder (a rule or an escalated band), and log settings changes as policy events with dates and owners; a discipline switch toggled casually is policy repealed silently.</p><p><b>Pattern 2 — the bundle parent has stock.</b> Someone ticked Maintain Stock on the parent, or receipts were booked against it (Ch. 3): now packs and children double-represent the same shelves and the stocktake disputes itself. Fix: zero and untick the parent through proper stock documents, recount the children, and re-teach the one-physical-truth principle.</p><p><b>Pattern 3 — bundle sold below cost for a quarter.</b> The silent-margin disease (Ch. 4): a child's cost rose; the parent's sealed price did not. Fix: the margin file with its monthly review — the loss was invisible precisely because no single record showed it.</p><p><b>Pattern 4 — blanket exists; orders ignore it.</b> Draws entered as plain orders at ad-hoc rates (Ch. 5): the agreement neither enforces its rate nor records its drawdown — the worst of both worlds, paper without teeth. Fix: re-reference the draws, correct rate history where the customer was overcharged against the agreement, and make blanket-reference part of the order discipline for agreement customers.</p><p><b>Pattern 5 — the tree is a graveyard.</b> Departed reps still active, their accounts orphaned, the rollup lying (Ch. 6). Fix: deactivate leavers, reassign their books the same week they exit — the tree review joins the offboarding checklist, because trees outlive staff only if someone prunes.</p><p><b>Pattern 6 — target theatre.</b> Ambitious targets, empty summaries (Ch. 6): assignment never became process, so the numbers float over a void and the review meeting reviews nothing. Fix: assignment-precedes-measurement, made structural — the Sales Team section expected on every order, checked in the weekly hygiene pass.</p><p><b>Pattern 7 — the partner's invoice is the ledger.</b> Commissions paid from claims because stamping lapsed (Ch. 7): over-payment or under-tracking, discovered whenever the relationship sours. Fix: reconstruct the quarter from transactions once, painfully; then stamp-at-entry and pay-from-the-report forever.</p><p><b>The seventh law — and the manual's close.</b> Selling 1-2: documents are downstream of masters. Selling 3: the pipeline is downstream of discipline. Selling 4: the order is the single source of fulfilment truth. Selling 5: the DN is where the word meets the world. Selling 6: tax and price are policy. Selling 7: the invoice ledger is the cash truth. And this module: <b>the advanced layer is structure that outlives staff — settings, bundles, blankets, trees and channels encode the firm's selling judgment so that it survives resignations, promotions, and busy Fridays.</b> A ZhiftERP Sales Professional is, finally, someone who installs judgment as structure: who makes the right thing the default thing, the visible thing, and the measured thing — and then lets the system be as relentless as no human can be. That is the certification. Go and sit the exam.</p>"),
]

